 "title": "The chamfer, moving — the direction you called the most interesting",
 "question": "The pixel look kept entirely, with the staircases cut off the diagonals: nothing blurred, nothing blended, exact palette. You said you would have to playtest it — this is the nearest thing to that I can put in front of you first. Worth iterating on, or is the difference too small to be worth the frame? Its corner rule has three settings and I can render the other two if this one is close.",
 "context_md": "Same world, same seed, same frames as the painted+ink card; scrub them together.\n\n- **A** — the game today at 3x.\n- **B** — the chamfer. At each corner of a cell, if the two neighbours across it are the same thing and it is not, that corner triangle is theirs. Read from what the cells *are*, never from their colour.\n\n**Watch the hillside** rather than the tree: a 45-degree slope stops being a staircase. In the crown it is subtle, which is itself part of the answer.\n\n**It was quietly deleting thin things and no longer does.** Cutting all four corners of a cell that has nothing orthogonally beside it takes the whole cell — at 2x that is the entire block, and a one-cell twig came through at **0.03** of its presence today. Such a cell is now left alone, on the grounds that it is not part of a staircase, which is the only thing this rule is for. That also fixes the lone leaf turning into an octagon.\n\n**It is the cheapest of the four new looks by a wide margin** — +3.8 ns per pixel against +25.2 for painted+ink — because it blends nothing.\n\nSame key as the others (`Shift`+`=` cycles the look), `Shift`+`[` for its corner rule, and nothing changes at normal zoom.",
 "kind": "frames",
 "blind": false,
